Directed by Carrie Cracknell in 2024 at the National Theatre in London.

I think the removal of the interchapters is quite notable; in the book, these help expand the book so it applies to a greater group; contrastingly, here we feel more honed in on the Joads. The sets and the costumes in particular were very immersive, and they helped to illustrate the intense poverty described in the book. This helped push Steinbeck's political message even further; before Casey's death, he describes having to 'see it,' and the audience here does literally see it. The minimalism of the National Theatre's Lyttleton stage allows a wide variety of locations to be convincingly portrayed. I enjoyed the performances widely and think especially Tom Joad, Ma Joad, and Casey were effective. Again, the scene where Tom tells Ma he is leaving effectively presents Steinbeck's political messaging. Casey as well was fantastic throughout, playing the thinking preacher acting as the moral and political centre of the play. The final scene was stressful and upsetting, and I think this works well considering the content of the scene; it really feels like a biblical curse or punishment. The music that frequently played between scenes was a great way of breaking up the scenes but also helped to set a wider mood of the period and perhaps substituted the role of the interchapters somewhat. The most striking thing about the play was how effectively and obviously it portrayed Steinbeck's inherently socialist political messaging. The sets, costumes, and the dialogue and emotions delivered by the characters bring the abject poverty and suffering of the novel to life. The obvious reaction to this true horror is the ideas presented by Casey, and in this way, even more effectively than the novel, the play pushes Steinbeck's own beliefs. It was overall just great, if you ask me; the play is the best adaptation I could imagine of the original text, portraying it relatively faithfully with great performances.

